## 2 · Memory architecture (MEM)

The harness distinguishes four kinds of memory. Each has an owner, a location, a lifetime and a size cap — an agent never receives "everything".

| ID | Memory | What it holds | Location | Lifetime | Written by | Read by | Cap (tiny) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| MEM-1 | **Short-term (working context)** | What one agent sees in one call: its SKILL prompt + the slice of long-term memory it needs + the current task | assembled in RAM by `build_context()`, never persisted | one call | context builder | the calling agent | writer: 4 Bible files + own outline slice + rolling summary ≤120 words |
| MEM-2 | **Long-term canonical (Story Bible)** | World rules, characters, timeline, mysteries, outline — the facts of the novel | `output/<slug>/bible/*.md` + `entities.json` | whole run and beyond | Worldbuilder, Character Architect, Plot Architect only | every agent (read-only) | world ≤350 words, ≤3 characters × 80 words, ≤8 timeline events, ≤3 mysteries |
| MEM-3 | **Long-term narrative (rolling summaries)** | What has already happened, compressed | `output/<slug>/summaries/chNN.md` + `running.md` | whole run | Summarizer (pure code + optional LLM) after each accepted chapter | Chapter Writer, Style Editor | each ≤60 words; `running.md` ≤ `prior_summary_max_words` |
| MEM-4 | **Episodic (critiques and decisions)** | Every critic verdict, every gate decision, every rewrite instruction | `output/<slug>/critiques/chNN.<critic>.json`, `logs/agents.jsonl` | whole run; append-only | critics, orchestrator | rewrite prompt (last critique only), humans, tests | last critique injected verbatim; older ones never re-fed |
| MEM-5 | **Procedural (state)** | Where the run is: stage, per-chapter status, iteration, cost so far | `output/<slug>/state.json` | whole run | orchestrator only | orchestrator (`resume`, `status`) | — |

Rules:

- **MEM-6 · No prose leaks.** Prior chapter text never enters a prompt; only MEM-3 summaries do. Test TST-3.
- **MEM-7 · Structured shadow of the Bible.** Alongside the Markdown, `bible/entities.json` holds machine-readable canon: `{characters:[{name, aliases[], role, first_chapter}], places:[…], tech:[{name, rule}], timeline:[{event, order}]}`. Critics check against the JSON; humans read the Markdown. Both are written by the same agent call (the SKILL requires both outputs).
- **MEM-8 · Memory budget.** `build_context()` reports the token estimate of each memory slice in the audit row (`ctx_bible_tokens`, `ctx_summary_tokens`, `ctx_task_tokens`). A slice over its cap raises `ContextBudgetExceeded` before the call is made.
- **MEM-9 · Forgetting is explicit.** Summaries are regenerated when a chapter is rewritten; the old summary is kept as `chNN.v<k>.md` so the episodic trail is complete.

